course: world
lesson: 189
title: Fighting fair: no straw men, no moving goalposts, no either-or
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Arguing is fine; arguing unfairly is the problem. Three unfair moves turn up in every family, classroom and playground.
explain: The straw man: twist what someone said into something sillier, then attack that. Mum says "less screen time on school nights"; Tom hears "never have fun again". Mum: "Less, Tom. L-E-S-S. Not 'never'. Not 'banished to a cave'."
explain: Moving the goalposts: every time the test is met, demand a harder one. Kai wants a photo of a ghost, then a video, then "real life"; Ruby changes the hide-and-seek rules every time she loses until there's no hiding at all.
explain: The either-or: pretend there are only two choices ("Either you feed the hamster or he starves!") when there is a rota on the fridge, literally right there.
explain: Fair fighting is simple: say back what the other person really said ("Do you mean...?"), agree what would change your mind before you start, and look for the middle options.
fact: A straw man repeats the other person's idea as something more extreme or sillier, then attacks that instead.
fact: Arguing fairly means repeating what the other person really said, then saying why you disagree.
fact: Liking cats doesn't mean hating dogs; asking "Do you mean...?" before arguing checks what someone really thinks.
fact: Moving the goalposts means changing what counts as proof each time the test is met.
fact: Agree what would change your mind before you argue; blurry photos and videos are easy to fake, so a good test is one that others can repeat.
fact: An either-or (false dilemma) gives two options when there are more, such as half and half, taking turns, or a rota.
fact: You can criticise a thing without attacking a person: "the pizza could be hotter" is not "your mum's a bad cook".
fact: Every pet needs daily care: cats need food, water, a litter tray and visits to the vet.
fact: A fair reply answers the real point; for school uniforms, one real point on the other side is that uniforms can be expensive for some families.
fact: Being scared of one thing doesn't mean being scared of everything; fears are specific.
fact: Agree the rules of a game before it starts; if you want a change, it starts next game.
fact: Words people use: "rota" (UK) means a list of whose turn it is; "literally" is often used to mean "really, obviously"; "eh" (Canada) means "right?"; "toque" (Canada) means a woolly hat; "bud" (Canada) means mate; "chur" (New Zealand) means thanks, cheers; "y'all" (US South) means you all; "fixin' to" (US South) means about to.
exercise: Mum: "Let's have less screen time on school nights." Tom: "So you want me to never have fun again?" What did Tom do? | Turned "less" into "never" | Repeated Mum's idea fairly | Agreed and moved on | Asked a fair question
exercise: Ella: "I think we should have cats at home." Her brother: "So you hate dogs?" What should he have asked? | "Do you mean you'd rather have a cat than a dog?" | "Why do you hate dogs so much?" | "Why are you always wrong?" | "Can we have no pets at all?"
exercise: Kai: "I'll believe ghosts are real if someone shows me a photo." Shown a blurry photo: "Needs to be a video." Shown a video: "Needs to be in real life." What is he doing? | Moving the goalposts | Running a fair and careful test | Using a straw man argument | Making an either-or choice
exercise: What should Kai have done from the start? | Say what evidence would really change his mind | Ask for more and more clips until he is sure, then decide | Refuse to look at any evidence | Believe the first photo
exercise: Why are blurry photos weak evidence for ghosts? | They are easy to fake or to misread | Ghosts can't be photographed in colour | Cameras can't take pictures at night | Photos always show the truth
exercise: Leo: "If we don't play tag, the whole break is wasted." What else is possible? | Half and half, or another game | Only tag, or nothing at all for the break | Only hide-and-seek, for ever and ever | Sitting still in a line all break
exercise: Ava: "I think the school dinners could be better." The dinner lady's son: "So you think my mum's a bad cook!" What did he do? | Twisted a general opinion into a personal attack | Repeated Ava's point fairly | Agreed with her reasons | Asked which dinners she meant
exercise: Mia: "Dogs need walks every day." Oscar: "So cats are better, because you never have to do anything with them." What do cats really need? | Food, water, a litter tray and the vet | Nothing at all, ever | Only a daily walk on a lead, like a dog | Only toys to play with
exercise: Freddie: "Either you feed the hamster or he starves!" What is the fair way to decide? | Check the rota, or do it together | Let the hamster go hungry for one day | Feed him twice a day just to be safe | Give the hamster away to a neighbour
exercise: Sam: "School uniforms help everyone feel equal." Lily: "So you think we should all be robots?" Which reply would be fair? | "Uniforms can be expensive for some families." | "So you want us all to be robots." | "You only like uniforms because you're boring." | "Either uniforms or total chaos."
exercise: Jamal: "If you're scared of spiders, you're scared of everything." What is true? | Fears are specific; one fear doesn't mean all fears | Fear of spiders means fear of everything | Brave people are never scared of anything | Nobody is really scared of spiders
exercise: Ruby changes the rules every time she loses at hide-and-seek. What is fair? | Agree the rules first; any change starts next game | Let whoever is losing change the rules | Change the rules every single round | Play with no rules at all
exercise: Which reply fights fair? | "You said less screen time. How much less?" | "So you want me to go and live in a cave, Mum?" | "You always ruin everything." | "Either no rules or no fun."
exercise: What is the opposite of moving the goalposts? | Agreeing what counts as proof before you start | Asking for more and more proof every single time | Hiding the goalposts | Changing the rules when you lose
test: Mum: "Less, Tom. L-E-S-S. Not 'never'." What is the fair next step for Tom? | Argue about how much less | Say she wants him to have no fun | Spell "never" back at her | Stop talking to her for a week
test: Ella: "Where did I say that? You've been arguing with a ghost for five minutes." Which trick has she spotted? | A straw man | Moving the goalposts | An either-or | A red herring
test: What would happen if Kai had said at the start, "A test that others can repeat in real life would convince me"? | Everyone would know what counts as proof | He would have to believe the blurry photo | Ghosts would appear when asked | The argument could never end
test: Ruby: "Or, wild idea, half and half? There are more than two games in the world, Leo." What did she do? | Broke the either-or with a middle option | Made a straw man of Leo's idea | Moved the goalposts | Attacked Leo instead of his idea about games
test: Ava: "I never said that! The pizza could be hotter, that's all. Your mum's custard is legendary." What did she do well? | Said exactly what she meant without attacking a person | Took back everything she had said | Agreed that the dinners are perfect | Blamed the dinner lady
test: A cousin in Canada: "Cats need nothing, eh? Mine needs a vet, a litter tray and a toque in winter, bud." Joke aside, what is the point? | Cats need daily care too | Cats need woolly hats to live | Dogs need no care at all | Cats are worse pets than dogs
test: Jade: "Or we look at the rota on the fridge, Freddie. It's literally right there." What did she offer? | A third option beyond the either-or | A straw man of Freddie's hamster idea | A way to let the hamster starve quietly | A way to avoid feeding him at all, ever
test: The teacher: "Robots? He said uniforms, Lily. Answer what he said." Which reply answers what he said? | "Uniforms can cost a lot for some families." | "So you want us all to be robots." | "You only like uniforms because you're boring." | "Either uniforms or chaos."
test: A Kiwi mate: "Scared of spiders, so scared of everything? Yeah nah, I did the biggest rollercoaster at Rainbow's End twice, chur." What is broken? | The idea that one fear means all fears | The idea that rollercoasters are fun | The idea that spiders are scary | The idea that New Zealand has theme parks
test: Ruby's friend: "So hide-and-seek with no hiding? That's just seek, Ruby. That's just looking at people." What is he laughing at? | Changing the rules mid-game to win | Ruby's really terrible hiding skills | Playing outside at break in the cold | The whole game of hide-and-seek itself
test: A kid from Texas: "Y'all fixin' to say I hate dogs 'cause I like cats? I never said that." What is the fair way to answer her? | Ask "Do you mean...?" and answer what she said | Say she hates dogs anyway, because she likes cats | Change the subject to horses | Say cats and dogs are the same
test: In a cartoon a referee carries the goal further away every time a player shoots. What does the cartoon show? | Moving the goalposts: changing the test once it's met | A fair way to make football harder | A straw man about football | An either-or about goals
exam: Identify the device employed where a person, on being presented with each successive piece of evidence that they had requested, demands evidence of a more exacting kind. | Moving the goalposts | A straw man | A false dilemma | An appeal to tradition
exam: It is given that a parent proposed a reduction in screen time on school nights and that the child replied that the parent wished him never to have fun again. Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reply? | It misrepresents the proposal as more extreme than it was | It restates the proposal accurately | It identifies a third option | It accepts the proposal without objection
exam: Select the response that is consistent with fair procedure in respect of the rules of a game. | The rules are agreed before play, and any change applies from the next game | The rules may be altered by whichever player is losing | The rules are altered after every round | The rules are determined by the loudest player
exam: Ascertain the principal deficiency of the assertion "Either you feed the hamster or he starves" where a rota of duties exists. | It omits further options, such as consulting the rota | It correctly identifies the only two options available | It attacks the character of the other party | It misstates the dietary needs of hamsters
